        "status": "OK",
        "asr_text": "One policy expert says U.S. restaurant tipping rose from about 10 to 15% in the early 1970s to about 20% by the late 1990s, and that today's digital screens can suggest tips as high as 35%. He describes the social pressure of choosing in front of a worker as guilt tipping. With a cash tip jar, the choice felt more private, while a digital screen can make it public.",
